### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer wants to see a repeatable learning process, because that tells them you can do it again with whatever technology they throw at you next. "I am a fast learner" means nothing without a method.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class LearningMethod {
    enum Signal { REPEATABLE, VAGUE }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean hasMethod, boolean hasResult) {
        return (hasMethod && hasResult) ? Signal.REPEATABLE : Signal.VAGUE;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true)); // REPEATABLE
    }
}
```

### How do you learn the fundamentals before the syntax?

For Kafka at National Bank of India, that means grasping topics, partitions, offsets, and consumer groups before writing a line of code. The mental model makes everything else click.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
import java.util.List;
public class FundamentalsFirst {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> kafkaConcepts = List.of(
            "Topics: logical channels for events",
            "Partitions: parallel processing units",
            "Offsets: position of each consumer in a partition",
            "Consumer groups: coordinate multiple readers"
        );
        System.out.println("Learn these BEFORE writing code:");
        kafkaConcepts.forEach(c -> System.out.println("  - " + c));
    }
}
```

### How do you build the smallest spike?

Publish one settlement event and consume it. That single working loop teaches more than hours of reading.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class MinimalSpike {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(MinimalSpike.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulated minimal spike
        String topic = "settlement-events";
        String event = "{transferId: TXN-5001, amount: 50000.00}";
        log.info("Published to " + topic + ": " + event);
        log.info("Consumed from " + topic + ": " + event);
        log.info("Spike passed: publish-consume loop works");
    }
}
```

```text
Published to settlement-events: {transferId: TXN-5001, amount: 50000.00}
Consumed from settlement-events: {transferId: TXN-5001, amount: 50000.00}
Spike passed: publish-consume loop works
```

### Why use authoritative sources over blog posts?

Money systems demand correctness. Official docs and reference examples are verified; random blogs may have subtle errors that cause data loss.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class SourceQuality {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Authoritative: official Kafka docs, reference consumer impl");
        System.out.println("Risky: random Medium post from 2019 with outdated API");
        System.out.println("For money systems: always authoritative first");
    }
}
```

### How do you apply incrementally in a banking context?

Route only audit events through the new bus first. Confirm reliability. Then move money-path events. Never trust a new technology with money on day one.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class IncrementalRollout {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Phase 1: Route audit events only (low risk)");
        System.out.println("Phase 2: Verify zero event loss over 1 week");
        System.out.println("Phase 3: Route settlement events (money path)");
        System.out.println("Phase 4: Verify exactly-once delivery for money");
    }
}
```

### How do you prove correctness before trusting money?

Confirm that every event published is consumed exactly once, with nothing lost or duplicated. "I learned it fast" is worthless if it loses a transaction.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class CorrectnessProof {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(CorrectnessProof.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int published = 1000;
        int consumed = 1000;
        int duplicates = 0;
        log.info("Published: " + published + ", Consumed: " + consumed + ", Duplicates: " + duplicates);
        log.info("Exactly-once verified: safe for money events");
    }
}
```

### How do you share knowledge with the team?

Document what you learned and run a short knowledge-sharing session. This multiplies the value of your ramp-up across the whole team.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class KnowledgeSharing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Artifacts:");
        System.out.println("  1. Internal wiki page: Kafka fundamentals + our patterns");
        System.out.println("  2. Runnable example project in team repo");
        System.out.println("  3. 30-minute team session: lessons learned + gotchas");
        System.out.println("Result: team ramp-up time cut from weeks to days");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "How long did it take you?" (give a specific timeline), "What was the hardest part?" (name a concrete challenge), and "Would you use the same technology again?" (show evaluative thinking).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class LearningFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("How long? -> 3 weeks from zero to production");
        System.out.println("Hardest part? -> Understanding exactly-once semantics for money");
        System.out.println("Use again? -> Yes for event streaming; might evaluate alternatives for other patterns");
    }
}
```
